According to [3], a Docker container is: 
1. a temporary file system; 
2. layered over an immutable fs (docker image); 
3. fully writable (copy-on-write); 
4. dropped at container's end of life (unless a commit is made); 
5. a network stack; 
6. with its own private address (by default in 172.17.x.x); 
7. a process group; 
8. one main process launched inside the container; 
9. all sub-processes SIGKILLed when the main process exits.
